        'mail.styles_head':             'Reply styles',
        'mail.styles_hint':             'Create as many ways of writing as you need \u2013 for example \u201cFormal\u201d, \u201cCasual\u201d or \u201cShort & to the point\u201d. A style only shapes HOW a text sounds (form of address, tone, signature); whether a reply is sent at all is decided by your rule alone. When replying in Outlook you pick the style from the drop-down, a rule uses the style set on it, otherwise the default applies. Every one of those drop-downs also offers \u201cchoose style automatically\u201d \u2013 the language model then picks a fitting one itself.',
        'mail.styles_hint_short':       'Several ways of writing \u2013 pick the one that applies on the \u201cMessage\u201d tab when you reply, or let the AI choose.',
        'mail.help_styles':             'The default style (\u25CF) applies wherever nothing else is chosen. You can also name a style in a rule\u2019s text, for example \u201cReply in the \u2018Formal\u2019 style\u201d \u2013 the name is resolved before the language model runs. If a style is selected in the rule form, that selection wins. A style NEVER triggers an action. Instead of a fixed style you can pick \u201cchoose style automatically\u201d anywhere: the language model then sees all your styles and decides based on the incoming message. When replying in Outlook, the one it used is shown above the draft.',
