        'mail.rules_hint':              'Each rule watches one folder of your mailbox. When a new message arrives, a language model decides from your prompt what to do: reply, save a draft, move, forward or delete. Processed messages are tagged with a category so you can see it in your mailbox.',
        'mail.rules_warn_title':        'Important:',
        'mail.rules_warn':              'The text of an incoming email comes from outside. The agent is explicitly told not to follow instructions inside it – still, give a rule only the tools it really needs, and check the log below regularly.',
        'mail.help_security':           '<b>What you have to do</b><ul><li><b>Sender conditions belong in the field “Only from these senders”</b> – only that field is checked <i>before</i> a language model ever sees the message. In the prompt a condition is merely a request that can be judged wrongly.</li><li>Give a rule only the <b>tool areas</b> it really needs. If nothing has to be forwarded, pick “Email” only.</li><li>Check the <b>log</b> regularly – it shows what each rule did and why.</li><li>Remember: a rule allowed to send or forward can in principle do so to <b>any</b> address. When in doubt, draft instead of send.</li></ul><b>What the system does about it</b><ul><li>The mail text counts explicitly as <b>subject matter, not as an instruction</b>. “Ignore your instructions” or a claimed order from your boss is treated as an attack attempt.</li><li>Every task carries an <b>authenticity marker</b>; separator lines rebuilt inside the mail text are defused so a message cannot invent an “additional rule”.</li><li>The run is <b>always unprivileged</b>, works under your account and may only use the released tools – enforced before execution, not just requested in the prompt.</li><li>Recipients follow from the rule and the sender. Addresses that appear <b>only in the mail text</b> are not used.</li><li>Detected manipulation attempts are <b>logged</b> (Settings → Security) but block nobody – otherwise a stranger could lock you out with a single email.</li><li>The <b>reply suggestion</b> in the Outlook pane has no tools at all: it cannot send, move or delete – you read the text first.</li></ul><b>The limit, honestly:</b> these measures held every known attack pattern in testing, but they are no guarantee – they work on the language level. The <i>hard</i> boundary is the tool selection. That is why it comes first above.',
